  "response": "그 멍해지는 순간이 어떤 건지, 지금 바로 설명하지 않아도 괜찮아요. 상업 촬영이 계속 들어오는 건 그 안을 들여다보지 않아도 되는 곳이니까요.\n\n그리고 한 달 남았다는 시간이 멍해지는 마음을 더 무겁게 만들 수도 있을 것 같아요. 지금 이 이야기에서 먼저 건드리고 싶은 부분이 있다면, 그것부터 천천히 가도 돼요.",
  "reasoning": "The user is a client in a counseling session. They're talking about applying for a personal exhibition grant with a one-month deadline. They need to select photos but feel frozen when opening files. They keep doing commercial work instead. They acknowledge it's a problem but say they need to talk more about why.\n\nWhat's happening here:\n- There's a practical pressure (deadline in one month)\n- There's an emotional block (feeling \"frozen\" when opening personal work files)\n- They're using commercial work as avoidance\n- They have self-awareness about the pattern\n- They explicitly say they need to talk more about \"why\" — they're not ready to jump into solutions yet\n\nWhat the counselor should do:\n- Acknowledge the awareness they already have\n- Honor their pace — they said they need more time to explore the \"why\"\n- Not rush into problem-solving or action plans\n- Maybe gently reflect what they've shared and invite them to continue at their own pace\n- One question at most, and only if it serves the conversation\n\nI should reflect back the tension between knowing what needs to be done and the freeze response, and perhaps gently name what they've said about needing more time. I shouldn't push them to explain the \"why\" immediately — they've set a boundary that they need to talk more about it. I can acknowledge that and leave space.\n\nLet me write a natural, warm response that validates their self-awareness and leaves room without forcing.\n",
